   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.2. Increased capacity of Member States to formulate and implement policies and strategies for creating decent work in the rural economy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government of Sudan implemented a program targeting young people with a view to build community resilience in Mayo Suburb of Khartoum through Improved Access to Water (SDN/20/03/JPN). This project applied the Employment-Intensive Investment Programme (EIIP) approach to improve rural infrastructure planning, rehabilitation, and maintenance. The project achieved the following results:
1.More than 260,000 residents benefitted from the rehabilitation of water access facilities and drainage systems in Mayo Suburb, Khartoum, creating job opportunities for 500 beneficiaries, 30% of which were women from the Mayo community. 
2. The project also focused on capacity building, particularly among young women and men. A total of 20 young individuals (4 women and 16 men) gained employment opportunities through the enhancement of their capacities and skills related to water and sanitation maintenance. Additionally, 2 persons with disabilities (PWDs) were included in business skill training programs focused on the rehabilitation of road and water facilities, as well as SIYB (Start and Improve Your Business) and IYB (Improve Your Business) training. 
3.Capacities of construction sector workers to safeguard themselves and others through occupational safety and health (OSH) principles, particularly in light of the COVID-19 crisis, were improved, making construction worksites safer to work in. Moreover, to ensure gender equality in the construction sector, 42% (126 out of the 300 beneficiaries) of workers were women who engaged in road infrastructure rehabilitation projects, creating employment opportunities for them. 
4. Local authorities and ministerial counterparts enhanced their capacities to contribute and develop evidence-based national investment planning. This facilitated effective planning for infrastructure investments and for the EIIP approach in preparation for recurring seasonal floods. With support of the Government of Japan (Project #: SDN/20/03/JPN), the ILO provided technical support in building the capacities of 15 Sudanese governmental officials, including 10 men and 5 women from Federal Ministry of Labour, Khartoum States and Kassala States, as well as implementing partners in the use of the EIIP&#x2019;s Integrated Rural Accessibility Planning (IRAP) methodologies.
ITCILO trained 20 Sudanese (13 men and 7 women) Government Officials of Ministries of Labour, Health and Defence and Civil Security and Senior officers on the importance and application of OSH in the construction, maintenance and rehabilitation of infrastructure works.
ILO supported the training of local government technical officers in the planning, design, implementation, supervision and monitoring of assets rehabilitation and maintenance using employment-intensive local resources-based approaches. ILO sponsored a study visit of 8 officials (5 men and 3 women) from Ministry of Labour and Labour Based Construction Unit (LBCU) to Jordan to update their knowledge in the planning, implementation and monitoring of EIIP activities. 
The ILO EIIP engineers provided training to 500 community members in the maintenance of 40 hand pumps rehabilitated to protect the community of 260,000 inhabitants against waterborne diseases; and an additional 200 beneficiaries were trained in road rehabilitation and maintenance. The beneficiaries were involved in rehabilitation of 3.25Km of community roads.</narrative>
   </description>
